## How this was built

Prices from the regulator index for every metropolitan area it covers from {{ v('data.fhfa.first_year') }} to {{ v('data.fhfa.latest_period') }}, incomes, rents and tenure from every one year census release from {{ v('data.acs.first_year') }} to {{ v('data.acs.last_year') }} with the {{ v('data.acs.gap_year') }} release carried as a gap, weekly mortgage rates, permits, population with its components, and {{ v('data.hmda.rows_in_analysis') }} mortgage originations from {{ v('data.hmda.first_year') }} to {{ v('data.hmda.last_year') }} went through {{ v('data.quarantine.rules') }} named quarantine rules into a dbt warehouse whose metric layer defines each number once and reconciles it at {{ v('metrics.grains') }} grains. Each chapter's plan was hashed before its estimate touched a real metro, and each estimator was first run on a simulated country of {{ v('sim.metros') }} metros where the answer is known, over {{ v('recovery.conditions') }} conditions and {{ v('recovery.seeds') }} seeds each. The story, the briefing, the site and the workbook all render from one manifest, so a number here is the number everywhere.

The method appendix, the definitions, the warehouse and the recovery study are on the [data page](data/), the calculator is on the [planner page](planner/), and every chart's SQL is one click from the chart.
